Subject: [PATCH v3 0/5] ui/spice: make non-GL damage handling stream friendly

Without gl=on, the simple spice display unions all damage into one box,
diffs it against a mirror in 32 pixel wide columns, sends one
QXL_DRAW_COPY per changed column run, and only pushes on the 30ms GUI
refresh timer. That starves spice-server's video stream detection: a
guest playing a 480x360 video at 30 fps makes spice-server 0.15.2 start
18-19 separate 32x360 H.264 streams, while thousands of small lossless
column draws keep flowing.

v3 adds one patch to v2 and changes nothing else. v2 was measured behind
a shaped 80 ms / 10 Mbit link and regressed badly whenever a region went
out as a bitmap rather than a stream: the server's ACK window counts
messages and not bytes, so one 1.2 MB drawable where the unpatched code
sends 60 KB columns makes the permitted backlog about twenty times
larger. Keypress-to-draw went from about 200 ms to about 1 s before a
stream forms, and from 2.1 s to 8.0 s for content streaming-video=filter
will not stream. Patch 5 caps the pixel payload of one DRAW_COPY and
sends larger damage as a stack of bands, each at a steady position and
size so stream detection can still work, per band rather than per
region.

The series:

 1. tracks up to 16 disjoint damage rectangles instead of one union,
    in a small spice independent helper with unit tests;
 2. uses the mirror diff only to trim each rectangle to the changed
    pixels (splitting on 32+ unchanged rows) and sends each result as
    one DRAW_COPY;
 3. pushes damage from a timer as soon as it arrives, paced by the
    existing max-refresh-rate option (default 30), instead of waiting
    for the refresh timer;
 4. also splits on 32+ unchanged columns, so side by side changes in
    the same rows stay apart;
 5. caps the bytes in one DRAW_COPY, banding damage that exceeds it.

Open question for patch 5, and the reason it is not presented as
settled: the budget trades two failure modes against each other. A
smaller budget bounds the backlog better and splits a region into more
drawables, which is what defeated stream detection to begin with. The
default is four times the unpatched code's largest message, which leaves
a 480x360 region in three bands; it is overridable at build time so the
rig can sweep it. The sweep has not been run, so the default is reasoned
rather than measured, and the band-versus-stream trade is the thing to
measure first.

Everything else from v2's cover letter still applies: spice-server caps
video streams at MAX_FPS 30 so pacing by max-refresh-rate costs no
stream frames by default; qemu defaults streaming-video to off, so the
stream benefits need filter or all, while patches 1-3 reduce draws,
bytes and latency with streaming off; Linux virtio-gpu guests from 6.8
on report full plane damage because ignore_damage_clips sticks once set,
which the row and column diff trims back and which a separate kernel fix
addresses; and the SPICE section in MAINTAINERS is orphaned, with ui/
being Graphics.

Michael Still (5):
  ui/spice: track a bounded list of damage rectangles
  ui/spice: send each damaged region as one DRAW_COPY
  ui/spice: push damage promptly, paced by max-refresh-rate
  ui/spice: also split damage on unchanged columns
  ui/spice: cap the bytes carried by one DRAW_COPY

